WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.960
A prompt is one component inside an engineered AI loop.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:10.980
The prompt shapes an interaction, but the loop coordinates repeated decisions, tool calls, observations, evaluations,

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.920
state changes, and terminal decisions.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:21.100
Reliable behavior comes from the whole harness: the task contract, state model, tool boundaries, evaluation logic,

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.720
policy enforcement, budgets, traces, and human oversight.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:30.660
Improving only the wording while ignoring the harness leaves important failure modes untouched.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.040
The disciplined loop begins with a contract.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:40.060
Define the intended outcome, permitted scope, observable completion evidence, constraints, required approvals, and limits.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:45.080
Initialize explicit state, then construct focused context for the current decision.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:51.540
Ask for one bounded next action, validate it against schema and policy, execute only an approved tool,

00:00:51.760 --> 00:01:00.000
record the observation, evaluate progress, update state, and decide whether to continue, pause, escalate, or terminate.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:03.740
The major boundaries are the safety rails of the architecture.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.160
Models propose; application policy authorizes.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.780
Tools perform bounded operations.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.480
Observations are recorded and validated.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.180
Evaluators test claims against criteria.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.240
Budgets limit execution.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.940
Human approvals control consequential actions.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.800
Traces support investigation and improvement.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:28.860
Terminal states make stopping explicit instead of leaving the run to fade out in free-form conversation.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.640
The common failure patterns are familiar by now.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.820
Vague definitions of done lead to false completion.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.360
Unbounded retries waste resources.

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:39.740
Activity is mistaken for progress.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.840
The generator is allowed to certify its own success.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.860
Entire histories are copied into every iteration.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.600
Tools are too broad.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.300
Untrusted content is treated as instruction.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.160
Uncertainty is hidden behind confident language.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:58.740
Deployment happens without evaluation, or cost is optimized while false completion rises.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.360
A useful loop is not the one that runs longest or sounds most autonomous.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:06.940
It is the one that reaches a justified outcome or stops safely.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:13.480
A well-designed AI loop continues only while the next action is permitted, evidence indicates meaningful progress,

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.580
resources remain within budget, and the path toward a valid terminal state is still credible.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:23.680
When those conditions fail, the system should preserve evidence and hand control back cleanly.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.500
That is the discipline that turns repeated model calls into engineering.
